Now I understand the issue. At line 128:

```cpp
if (required_gps && !workers.worker<GpsConnector>(k_worker_gps_connector)->active()) {
    return STATUS_ERROR_GPS;
}
```

The GPS error is shown when `required_gps` is true AND the GPS connector is not `active()`. 

During boot, the GPS connector goes through multiple baud rate attempts, returning `false` each time until it finally detects the NMEA-only mode. During this period (which takes about 10 seconds from the log), the GPS is not active, so the error message is displayed.


The real problem is that the Drive screen enters about 1.2 seconds before the GPS finishes its detection sequence, triggering this false alarm. I should look into whether we can add a grace period before showing the error, check if the Drive screen actually needs GPS at startup, or see if there's already a mechanism in place to handle this boot-time delay. Looking at the GPS initialization sequence from the logs, it takes about 11.6 seconds to fully activate, so I should add a grace period similar to the 6-second one already in place for the geiger counter—maybe around 12-13 seconds to give the GPS enough time to initialize before showing the error message to the user.

But the user's complaint about "always" getting a GPS error suggests this might be happening on multiple screens during boot, not just the brief 1.2-second window I'm seeing in this particular log. Let me check what other screens require GPS and when they're being displayed.

